1
00:00:00,078 --> 00:00:02,640
Have you ever asked Claude Code to make a website?

2
00:00:02,766 --> 00:00:06,960
And when you saw the website it built, you got the feeling that you'd seen one like this before.

3
00:00:07,214 --> 00:00:14,000
Since we're a software company, we have built a lot of sites and we have seen that most of the AI generated sites end up looking the same.

4
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:20,292
But the issue isn't with the models, it's because you're not following a structured process to actually get good designs out of them.

5
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:27,520
So in this video, we're going to teach you our 6-stage process that you can use to make incredibly beautiful websites using Claude Code.

6
00:00:27,694 --> 00:00:32,560
If you've never opened Claude Code before, we're going to be teaching you how to use Claude Code from the start.

7
00:00:32,686 --> 00:00:40,640
And if you've been using Claude Code every day, there's something for you too, because we're also going to be showing you a skill that can take your normal AI-built

8
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:43,972
websites and turn them into amazing cinematic scrollable websites.

9
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:49,028
If you feel like you already know something, the timestamps are below, so you can jump between the sections.

10
00:00:49,518 --> 00:00:53,280
But before we start, we're going to set up the apps that we'll be using in this process.

11
00:00:53,440 --> 00:01:01,040
The first tool is Claude Code, which is like ChatGPT or the Claude Web App, but it has the ability to actually control your whole computer.

12
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:04,400
But in order to run Claude Code, we need something called a terminal.

13
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:06,148
For that, we are going to use Warp.

14
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:11,200
And since Claude Code can actually create and edit files on your computer, we need a way to view them.

15
00:01:11,342 --> 00:01:13,188
For that, we're going to be using VS Code.

16
00:01:13,518 --> 00:01:16,000
Now we're going to start the setup by installing Warp.

17
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:24,000
To install it, you need to download Warp from its website and install it on your computer for your specific OS, whether it is Mac OS or Windows.

18
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:27,440
After it is installed, you're going to open it up and see something like this.

19
00:01:27,566 --> 00:01:31,812
But before we start working with Claud Code, we need to open a folder inside Warp.

20
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:35,680
We're doing this because we need to run Claud code inside a folder on our computer.

21
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:39,360
You have the option to use an existing folder or create a new one.

22
00:01:39,502 --> 00:01:42,148
We're going to create this new folder and open it inside Warp.

23
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:47,360
Now the next step is to install Claude Code and on its website, it's going to give you this install button.

24
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:51,840
But instead of installing this, you're going to scroll down and choose the terminal option here.

25
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,200
Again, we've got installation commands for both Windows and Mac.

26
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:01,316
You just need to copy this command and paste it into Warp, and after that, you'll need to press enter and it's going to start installing.

27
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:07,120
Once it's installed, you need to type Claude, and after you press enter, it's going to open up Claude Code for you.

28
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:10,960
After that, you can start prompting it and it can actually do stuff on your computer.

29
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:16,160
For example, we can ask Claude Code to create a test file for us and it's going to go ahead and create that.

30
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:24,240
Now to open and view that file, we need to install VS Code from its site. After it's installed, you're going to open up VS Code and get a window like this.

31
00:02:24,430 --> 00:02:27,428
Here, we need to open the same folder we opened up in Warp.

32
00:02:27,662 --> 00:02:31,040
After opening it, you can see the test file Claude created right here.

33
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:33,680
You can also ask Claude to add something in the file.

34
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:42,160
For example, if we ask it to write this text inside, it's going to insert it. And if we check it inside VS Code, then we can see that our text is present inside the file.

35
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:50,480
Now in Warp, we've got these tabs on the side. To open a new tab, you can press Command T and it'll create this new tab, which is also open inside the same folder.

36
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:56,880
We can start another Claude Code session in this tab, but if we want to work in a different folder, we need to move into that folder first.

37
00:02:57,134 --> 00:03:02,960
If Claude Code is already running in the tab, you can use the exit command to close the session and return to the terminal.

38
00:03:03,086 --> 00:03:07,680
Now you already saw how we opened up this folder in Warp, but that isn't the correct way to do that.

39
00:03:07,790 --> 00:03:09,920
Now we're going to be using terminal commands.

40
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:14,884
These let us move between folders without opening each folder in the macOS menu separately.

41
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:20,560
Now the first command is ls, which lists all the files and folders in the folder we're currently in.

42
00:03:20,654 --> 00:03:22,560
Here, we're inside this demo folder.

43
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:29,440
To go back to documents, which is the folder containing the demo folder, we use the CD command followed by a space and two dots.

44
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:34,080
Now we're inside documents and running ls again shows us the files and folders here.

45
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:39,440
To create a folder for a new project, we use the mkdir command followed by the folder name.

46
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:44,000
Now if we run ls again, we can see that the folder we created is inside documents.

47
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:47,520
To move into it, we use the CD command followed by the folder name.

48
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:53,440
Now we're inside the new folder we just created, and typing Claude opens a new Claude Code session in this folder.

49
00:03:53,806 --> 00:03:58,640
Now that we've got Claude code running, we're going to look at the features you'll use while working on this project.

50
00:03:58,766 --> 00:04:02,880
You can type a forward slash inside Claude code to see the available commands.

51
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:06,640
There are many commands available, but we'll discuss the most used ones for now.

52
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:09,920
The model command lets you choose which Claude model you're working with.

53
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:15,300
For this video, we'll be using Opus 5.5 because this model is the best at putting out great designs.

54
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:20,160
But for other simple tasks, you can use Sonnet and for more complex tasks, you can use Fable.

55
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:24,480
While Claude is working, you can press escape to interrupt it and give it a correction.

56
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:30,880
And if you wanted to look at a specific file, you can type the at symbol and type the name of that file to include it in your prompt.

57
00:04:31,216 --> 00:04:40,016
As you keep working, the conversation and the files Claude reads take up its context window, which is basically how much text it can hold inside it during one chat session.

58
00:04:40,176 --> 00:04:43,216
The context command shows how much of that space you're using.

59
00:04:43,376 --> 00:04:47,856
If the conversation gets too long, the compact command summarizes it to free up space.

60
00:04:48,016 --> 00:04:50,256
You can also tell it what to keep in that summary.

61
00:04:50,350 --> 00:04:54,576
But if you're moving on to a separate task, the clear command starts a fresh conversation.

62
00:04:54,798 --> 00:05:02,656
When you're working in a Claud Code chat, if you exit and reopen Cloud Code, unlike the Claude web app, the chats aren't saved and the session is going to start fresh.

63
00:05:02,862 --> 00:05:09,056
But Claud Code allows you to save a chat with the rename command and to go back to that chat, you can use the resume command.

64
00:05:09,216 --> 00:05:15,456
When you use this command in a new session, it's going to show you the session that you saved and you can just open that and continue working.

65
00:05:15,694 --> 00:05:23,296
If Claude makes edits you want to undo, then you can press the escape button twice and go back to a previous message where everything was working fine.

66
00:05:23,456 --> 00:05:32,772
So the basic workflow is very simple. You open Claude code inside your project, tell it what you want to build, and then use these commands whenever you need more control over how it works.

67
00:05:33,486 --> 00:05:42,896
Now, in this video, we're going to build a demo website for an architecture design firm called Forma, and we're going to build this site using the six stages that we mentioned at the start.

68
00:05:43,150 --> 00:05:45,376
Starting off with the first stage, which is planning.

69
00:05:45,536 --> 00:05:49,136
For that, we're going to use the plan mode, which is built inside Claude Code.

70
00:05:49,296 --> 00:05:56,576
What's different with plan mode is that it stops Claude Code from actually making or editing any files, which forces it to only talk to you.

71
00:05:56,736 --> 00:06:02,576
This encourages Claude Code to think about things more deeply and ask you specific questions about what you want to build.

72
00:06:02,736 --> 00:06:08,548
This helps you plan the important details before Claude Code starts building and avoids wasting time on the wrong thing.

73
00:06:08,846 --> 00:06:13,776
To start planning, you can either use the plan command or press shift tab to activate plan mode.

74
00:06:13,936 --> 00:06:18,656
After that, you can give it an initial prompt where you can tell it what you are building the landing page for.

75
00:06:18,816 --> 00:06:21,296
Then it's going to start asking you questions about it.

76
00:06:21,456 --> 00:06:25,376
The first question it asked us was which tech stack we wanted to build this site with.

77
00:06:25,536 --> 00:06:28,256
That just means what tools we're going to use to build the site.

78
00:06:28,398 --> 00:06:34,656
We're going to use Next.js, which gives you the simplest and quickest way to build these beautiful sites, so you can just choose that.

79
00:06:34,816 --> 00:06:41,856
After that, it asked us which sections needed to be added inside the landing page, which means it was asking about the structure of the site.

80
00:06:42,016 --> 00:06:48,576
After answering that, it asked some other questions, such as the design style and whether we had the content to put on the site or not.

81
00:06:48,736 --> 00:06:55,696
When we had answered all the questions, Claude presented us with this plan that included what needed to be built and how it was going to build it.

82
00:06:55,856 --> 00:07:00,736
But before we implement the plan, it would be great if you subscribe to the channel and hit the hype button.

83
00:07:00,896 --> 00:07:03,556
This small gesture of support goes a long way for us.

84
00:07:04,014 --> 00:07:07,456
So after the plan is finalized, Claude code needs to execute it.

85
00:07:07,616 --> 00:07:15,136
You can either enable auto mode and let it start the execution, or you can write something in the third option if you want to suggest some change in the plan.

86
00:07:15,296 --> 00:07:20,964
We selected the first option and it started the implementation, writing the code that it needed to make the landing page.

87
00:07:21,136 --> 00:07:27,696
Once it was finished building, it gave us the summary of what it had built. Now, in order to run the website, you can just ask Claude to run it.

88
00:07:27,856 --> 00:07:31,616
Claude will run it in the background and it'll give you a link to open the website.

89
00:07:31,776 --> 00:07:39,536
You cannot share this link with anyone else because the website is running on your computer right now. Also, the link will only work until the Claude session is running.

90
00:07:39,696 --> 00:07:41,924
Once you exit it, the link is going to stop working.

91
00:07:42,318 --> 00:07:44,816
So this is what Claude Code finally came up with.

92
00:07:44,976 --> 00:07:53,536
The landing page was built according to the plan, and Claude Code had also added images from the internet, but the layout still had that familiar AI generated look.

93
00:07:53,696 --> 00:08:00,036
The page had the content we'd asked for, but we wanted a different design that didn't look like it was generated by an AI model.

94
00:08:00,302 --> 00:08:07,136
But before we start changing it, we need to talk about the second stage. Here we'll save the project details in a file called the Claude.md.

95
00:08:07,296 --> 00:08:13,616
As you already know, when you open a new Claude code session, it has no memory about what has previously happened in that folder.

96
00:08:13,710 --> 00:08:17,776
So you need a way to remind it about important things whenever a new chat is started.

97
00:08:18,030 --> 00:08:24,804
The Claude.md helps here by injecting whatever is inside it into the context window of the model at the very start of the chat.

98
00:08:25,006 --> 00:08:30,496
Now you can write the things inside the file yourself, but if you want Claude Code to create it, you can use the init command.

99
00:08:30,656 --> 00:08:36,416
After that, Claude examines the project and prepares a starter Claude.md based on what it finds inside.

100
00:08:36,622 --> 00:08:43,216
But if you use init on a simple project, then Claude Code is just going to write how to start the app and what is inside the code.

101
00:08:43,376 --> 00:08:50,096
It's going to miss out the important information, which is why we are building that site and the purpose of each section in the website.

102
00:08:50,256 --> 00:08:57,216
After that, when we make changes, it should keep in mind that the website's main goal is to generate leads for our architecture design firm.

103
00:08:57,326 --> 00:09:00,256
And each section's content and design should reflect that.

104
00:09:00,416 --> 00:09:04,736
In our project, after we ran the init command, the Claude.md file looked like this.

105
00:09:04,896 --> 00:09:10,976
It already contained information about how to start the website, what the project was, and how the different files were organized.

106
00:09:11,136 --> 00:09:15,936
But as already mentioned, it missed the business context for the website, so we added that context ourselves.

107
00:09:16,110 --> 00:09:21,376
To add something to Claude.md, you can simply tell Claude what information you wanted to add to the file.

108
00:09:21,536 --> 00:09:27,376
In our case, we asked Claude to add the business context for Forma, which included what the business actually was,

109
00:09:27,630 --> 00:09:34,148
why a customer would come to the website, and what the website needed to achieve for the business by converting a customer into a client.

110
00:09:34,336 --> 00:09:39,296
After that, it added a much more detailed description of the business inside the Claude.md.

111
00:09:39,456 --> 00:09:45,616
Now you might think, why would we need to add this information inside the Claude.md and why is it an important stage?

112
00:09:45,776 --> 00:09:51,376
As you already saw, we weren't satisfied with the website that Claude Code had made in the start, and we're going to change it.

113
00:09:51,536 --> 00:10:01,476
Having a consistent Claude.md maintains the intention of the website even if we make repeated changes, which in our case is to convert visitors of the page into sales on the website.

114
00:10:01,870 --> 00:10:10,052
Now we already stated that the landing page looked like it was AI generated, and this is why we move on to the third stage in which we'll use a skill called Hallmark.

115
00:10:10,336 --> 00:10:16,976
Those of you that don't know, a skill in Claude Code is basically a file with a set of instructions for a particular kind of work.

116
00:10:17,070 --> 00:10:20,576
And using these, you can instruct Claude Code to work in a certain way.

117
00:10:20,736 --> 00:10:24,496
You can use a skill anytime by typing its name just like a slash command.

118
00:10:24,686 --> 00:10:31,140
The Hallmark skill gives Claude Code the rules for moving away from design patterns that are most commonly used by AI agents.

119
00:10:31,406 --> 00:10:35,748
For existing projects, Claude Code will check its work against the rules of Hallmark.

120
00:10:35,950 --> 00:10:39,376
In this way, we can fix the generic design that was created before.

121
00:10:39,630 --> 00:10:45,776
In order to install it, you need the installation command for the Hallmark website, which is going to be listed in the description below.

122
00:10:45,936 --> 00:10:54,896
You can run the command in your terminal, but you don't need to close Claud Code because it also has a terminal mode which lets you run terminal commands directly from its input box.

123
00:10:54,990 --> 00:11:03,696
You need to type an exclamation mark at the start of the input, then paste the command and run it. Once it's installed, you can use the Hallmark command and ask it to rebuild the site.

124
00:11:03,856 --> 00:11:06,896
Initially it asked us about the type of visual style we want.

125
00:11:07,056 --> 00:11:14,256
It didn't ask any other business related questions about our website because we had already defined our product in the Claude.md file.

126
00:11:14,416 --> 00:11:20,644
After we told it the visual style, Claude worked through the landing page and it made all the design changes according to the skill.

127
00:11:20,846 --> 00:11:23,936
The landing page it produced is very different from the first version.

128
00:11:24,096 --> 00:11:30,256
The page no longer follows the original arrangement and it feels like the design is much more sophisticated and carefully planned.

129
00:11:30,416 --> 00:11:35,776
The spacing and page layout is more refined, and most importantly, this didn't look AI generated.

130
00:11:35,982 --> 00:11:42,176
Now, even though the skill helped a lot, it still wasn't able to solve one of the greatest issues in designing with AI models.

131
00:11:42,336 --> 00:11:46,336
This is why we move on to the fourth stage, which is the design.md file.

132
00:11:46,496 --> 00:11:52,176
The problem is that we can use design skills to change the layout, spacing, and the elements placed on the website,

133
00:11:52,256 --> 00:11:56,576
but the AI models still use the default colors and fonts that they have been trained on.

134
00:11:56,750 --> 00:12:04,896
For example, Claude's Opus model always defaults to warm cream or off-white colors for making websites, no matter which design skill you use with them.

135
00:12:05,056 --> 00:12:15,536
In order to overcome this behavior of AI models, you can use a design.md file. It's like a Claude.md file, but it has details about the colors, fonts, and the spacing we need in our website.

136
00:12:15,696 --> 00:12:20,580
This file helps you lock down these things so that they override the model's default design choices.

137
00:12:20,896 --> 00:12:28,416
Once we have a design.md, every time we change or add elements of our website, Claude will have consistent reference about our visual rules.

138
00:12:28,814 --> 00:12:32,560
So the colors and fonts can remain the same even if you revamp the whole site.

139
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:35,840
You don't have to build these design.md files by yourself.

140
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:42,372
Instead, there are places where you can browse different design.md files, choose the one you like and add it in your project.

141
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:46,640
Here, we'll use aura.build to get our design.md file.

142
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:52,720
You can browse through a big collection of these files, and if you like one, you can open it up and click on the download button.

143
00:12:52,880 --> 00:12:59,680
Now here we used a pre-built design.md file, but if you want to create a design that is completely unique for your business branding,

144
00:12:59,840 --> 00:13:03,600
we have a design.md planner skill which we have in our design system.

145
00:13:03,760 --> 00:13:08,480
That system is available for you to use in AI Labs Pro, which is our community.

146
00:13:08,654 --> 00:13:13,440
So if you found value in what we do and want to support the channel, this is the best way to do it.

147
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:14,884
The links in the description.

148
00:13:15,182 --> 00:13:21,680
After it is downloaded, we need to copy the downloaded file from downloads and paste it into the folder where you're building the website.

149
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:28,080
Once it is in the project folder, you can rename it to design.md so that you can refer to it by this name in your prompt.

150
00:13:28,302 --> 00:13:34,320
To apply the style, you can just ask Claude Code to read the design.md and revamp the website according to it.

151
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:38,880
In the same prompt, you can tell it to preserve the existing page structure and animations as well.

152
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:43,760
After that, it's going to start editing the files again, and after it's done, it's going to present the changed site.

153
00:13:43,886 --> 00:13:50,116
The updated page keeps the same overall structure as before, but the color scheme, fonts, and spacing has completely changed.

154
00:13:50,240 --> 00:13:56,880
If we ask Claude Code to make any other changes in the layout of the website, or if we ask it to add new elements to the site,

155
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:01,600
it's going to stick to these design rules that are written inside the design.md file.

156
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:05,636
This file is the main thing that helps us in keeping consistent visual branding.

157
00:14:05,998 --> 00:14:11,120
Now that our website is ready, we need to put it on the internet so that other people can start using it.

158
00:14:11,280 --> 00:14:15,876
Until now, we have been running the project on our computer using the local host links.

159
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,360
So in the fifth stage, we'll see how we can make our website live.

160
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:22,788
In order to deploy the website on the internet, we'll be using Viral.

161
00:14:22,960 --> 00:14:25,200
But we can't just deploy it with Versal alone.

162
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:33,920
We need to use GitHub here as well. GitHub is basically a Google Drive for storing code and it has another feature that you need to start using while building these sites.

163
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:37,840
When you add a new feature, there is a risk that some old feature might break.

164
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:42,960
So we need to save our work before adding something new. This is exactly what GitHub helps you do.

165
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:48,868
Each save checkpoint is called a commit, and you can easily move between these commits just by asking Claude code.

166
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:54,532
So if you implement a new feature in your app and it has some problem, you can just go back to the last commit.

167
00:14:54,894 --> 00:14:59,360
Now that we know what GitHub is, we can use it to create a commit in our landing page project.

168
00:14:59,470 --> 00:15:03,040
You can ask Claude to initialize GitHub in the project and create a commit.

169
00:15:03,214 --> 00:15:06,960
Now we have a commit, and when you add new features, you should create new commits.

170
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:16,068
After adding new features, if you ever think that the new features you've added are not useful, you can just come back to the first commit and it will just revert back to the original one.

171
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:22,160
As we have told you, GitHub is necessary for deployment on Versal. It helps with making new features live easily.

172
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:28,996
When you add a new feature and upload it to GitHub, it easily gets synced by Versal and you can see the changes on your website immediately.

173
00:15:29,200 --> 00:15:31,040
You first make commits on your computer.

174
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:32,560
These are called local commits.

175
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:38,276
Once these commits are sent to GitHub inside the code folder called the repository, they become remote commits.

176
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:45,508
When a new remote commit is made, Versal is going to read your changes and then display the updated landing page on your actual website link.

177
00:15:45,646 --> 00:15:53,188
So if we want to make changes to our website in the future, we can just update the files on GitHub and the published website will also have the new changes.

178
00:15:53,550 --> 00:15:57,348
In order to set up GitHub, the first thing that you need is a GitHub account.

179
00:15:57,440 --> 00:16:01,040
You can create a new account using Google or login if you already have one.

180
00:16:01,166 --> 00:16:08,292
Once you have a GitHub account ready, you can use the GitHub CLI to upload the project, which lets you control GitHub with terminal commands.

181
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:13,440
You can ask Claude to install and set it up, and once it is installed, it will ask you to run a command.

182
