215
00:19:03,854 --> 00:19:11,808
Early in the video, we talked about GitHub commits and how they are useful to revert changes, but there is one more tool inside GitHub that helps you create new

216
00:19:11,808 --> 00:19:14,448
features without worrying if it might break the app or not.

217
00:19:14,608 --> 00:19:16,388
This tool is called Git Branches.

218
00:19:16,528 --> 00:19:24,996
It allows you to make copies of your original project and each copy is called a branch. The project you started with is called the main branch. Each branch keeps its own commits.

219
00:19:25,168 --> 00:19:30,688
The main branch stays the same, keeping your original project untouched until you add your commits back to it.

220
00:19:30,862 --> 00:19:39,808
This combining of branches is called merging, and it adds the commits of a new branch to the commits of the main branch. Now, GitHub has some strictness when it comes to merging branches.

221
00:19:40,014 --> 00:19:45,284
So when you merge a branch, GitHub forces you to review the new branch, and this review is called a pull request.

222
00:19:45,488 --> 00:19:50,368
Once you have reviewed the changes and have made sure that there is no conflict, you can finally merge them.

223
00:19:50,478 --> 00:19:56,368
